theory.intro:
A **multistage experiment** has two or more steps, such as tossing a coin twice or drawing two marbles. A **two-way array** or a **tree diagram** shows every possible outcome. Multiply along the branches of a path, and add the paths that suit the event.

[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\node[left] at (O) {Start};
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A)$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(A')$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B \mid A)$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B' \mid A)$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B \mid A')$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below left, font=\small, inner sep=2pt] {$P(B' \mid A')$};
\node[above right, inner sep=3pt] at (N1) {$A$};
\node[below right, inner sep=3pt] at (N2) {$A'$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$B$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$B'$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$B$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$B'$};
\node[font=\small] at (1.2,3.4) {Stage 1};
\node[font=\small] at (3.7,3.4) {Stage 2};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]

foundation.question_text:
A fair coin is tossed and a fair six-sided die is rolled.
The tree diagram shows the possible outcomes, where $H$ is heads, $T$ is tails, $E$ is an even number, and $O$ is an odd number.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{2}$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$H$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$T$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$E$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$O$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$E$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$O$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the probability of obtaining a tail and an even number, as a fraction in simplest form.

A bag contains $3$ red marbles and $4$ blue marbles.
Two marbles are drawn one after the other **without replacement**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{7}$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{4}{7}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{2}{6}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{4}{6}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{6}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{6}$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$R$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$B$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$R$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$B$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$R$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$B$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the probability that both marbles drawn are red, as a fraction in simplest form.

A box contains $2$ caramel chocolates ($C$) and $3$ mint chocolates ($M$).
Two chocolates are selected at random **without replacement**.
[tikz]
\begin{tikzpicture}[every node/.style={font=\large}, scale=0.95]
\coordinate (O) at (0,0);
\coordinate (N1) at (2.4,1.4);
\coordinate (N2) at (2.4,-1.4);
\coordinate (E1) at (5.0,2.2);
\coordinate (E2) at (5.0,0.6);
\coordinate (E3) at (5.0,-0.6);
\coordinate (E4) at (5.0,-2.2);
\draw (O) -- (N1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{2}{5}$};
\draw (O) -- (N2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{5}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E1) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{1}{4}$};
\draw (N1) -- (E2) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{3}{4}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E3) node[midway, above, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{2}{4}$};
\draw (N2) -- (E4) node[midway, below, fill=white, font=\small] {$\frac{2}{4}$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N1) {$C$};
\node[fill=white, inner sep=2pt] at (N2) {$M$};
\node[right] at (E1) {$C$};
\node[right] at (E2) {$M$};
\node[right] at (E3) {$C$};
\node[right] at (E4) {$M$};
\end{tikzpicture}
[/tikz]
Find the probability of choosing one caramel and one mint chocolate, in any order, as a fraction in simplest form.

